menuconfig UBI
        bool "Unsorted Block Images (UBI)"
        select FLASH
        select FLASH_MAP
        select CRC
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_CMAC
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_HKDF
        select PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_256
        select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_AES
        select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_DERIVE
        help
          Volume manager for raw flash: maps logical erase blocks onto
          physical ones, spreads wear, survives power loss and authenticates
          its own metadata with AES-CMAC. Application data is neither
          encrypted nor authenticated.

if UBI

config UBI_MAX_NR_OF_VOLUMES
        int "Maximum number of volumes"
        default 4
        range 1 64
        help
          Volumes one device can hold. A build that allows fewer than a
          device holds refuses it with -ENOTSUP, so raise this across an
          update, never lower it.

config UBI_WEAR_LEVELING_THRESHOLD
        int "Erase count spread that makes relocation worthwhile"
        default 256
        range 64 512
        help
          A block in use this many erases below the free block it would move
          onto is worth relocating. Writes take the most worn free block
          within this many erases of the least worn one, relocation within
          twice as many. Lower values wear more evenly and copy more.

config UBI_PROTECTION_CYCLES
        int "Erases a freshly allocated block is left alone for"
        default 64
        range 0 255
        help
          Relocation leaves a freshly allocated block alone for this many
          erases anywhere on the device, as its data is likely to be
          rewritten soon. Zero turns this off. Kept in RAM, so every block
          starts unprotected at attach. Below 16, relocation can end up
          chasing the most often rewritten data.

config UBI_STATE_CHECK_INTERVAL
        int "Flash writes between state checks"
        default 64
        range 1 1024
        help
          The state callback is consulted at attach and, once this many flash
          writes have gone by, before the next operation that writes.

config UBI_ERASE_INVALIDATES_HEADERS
        bool "Clear the header magics before every erase"
        default y
        help
          An erase cut short can leave a block's headers intact while part of
          its data is gone. Zeroing the magic of each header before the erase
          makes the next attach erase such a block rather than take it back.

          Needs flash that accepts a second write over programmed bytes. Turn
          it off on flash with ECC or a write-once rule; left on there, the
          first refused write is logged and erases carry on without it until
          the next attach.

config UBI_IO_CHUNK_SIZE
        int "Bytes read or copied at a time"
        default 128
        range 64 4096
        help
          Attach, relocation and data checks go through the flash in pieces
          of this size, held on the stack of the calling thread. Must be a
          multiple of 64. Larger pieces take fewer flash transactions and
          more stack.

config UBI_VERIFY_ON_READ
        bool "Verify the VID header before every read"
        help
          Every ubi_leb_read() re-reads and authenticates the volume
          identifier header and checks that it names the block being read.
          Guards against tampering at run time, at the cost of a flash read
          and a CMAC per call. Data checksums are checked at attach only.

config UBI_SELF_CHECKS
        bool "Check the library's own bookkeeping at run time"
        help
          After every call, checks that every mapped logical block names a
          physical block in use and every physical block in use backs exactly
          one logical block; also checks allocation and relocation choices.
          Takes a bitmap of one bit per block from the heap at attach. Meant
          for tests and bring-up.

config UBI_SHELL
        bool "Diagnostic shell commands"
        depends on SHELL
        help
          Adds a "ubi" shell command that lists the attached devices and,
          for each, shows the device information, the volumes, which
          physical block backs every logical one, and the state and erase
          count of every physical block. Read-only: it writes nothing to
          the flash and changes no state. Devices are named by the number
          of their partition. Costs one pointer in the handle.

module = UBI
module-str = ubi
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # UBI
